IMPORT prelude

-- Supply of Goods Act 1982 (Singapore) -- the terms implied when goods change hands under a contract that is NOT a sale of goods
-- and NOT a hire-purchase: barter and part-exchange, goods supplied with work (a contractor's materials), and the hire or rental of
-- goods. Covered: which contracts the Act reaches (ss 1, 6, 18(1) "goods", 19(2)), title and quiet possession (ss 2, 7), description
-- (ss 3, 8), satisfactory quality and fitness for purpose (ss 4, 9), sample (ss 5, 10), the slight-breach rule for non-consumers
-- (ss 5A, 10A, 18(5)) and exclusion of implied terms (s 11).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/SGA1982.txt. The deposit's cover says the revised
-- edition "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021". The
-- latest amendment its Legislative History lists is Act 44 of 1996 (Supply of Goods (Amendment) Act 1996, commencement 1 January
-- 1997). The Act is the UK Supply of Goods and Services Act 1982, applied in Singapore from 12 November 1993 by the Application of
-- English Law Act 1993; Part 2 (services, ss 12 to 16) is marked "not applicable".
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Chosen for its everyday-life relevance: renting a car or a party tent, trading in an old phone for a new one, or a
-- renovation contractor fitting tiles and a water heater are not sales of goods, and this Act is where the person on the receiving
-- end gets the same quality and fitness protection a buyer has. The whole of Part 1 is short, so it is nearly all encoded.
-- Not encoded: the credit-broker limbs of ss 4(4)(b) and 9(4)(b), terms annexed by usage (ss 4(7), 9(7)), the bailor's right to
-- repossess (s 7(3)), the quality aspects of s 18(3) beyond a comment, other enactments preserved by s 11(3), and the Unfair
-- Contract Terms Act 1977, to which s 11(1) defers (deposited separately; not read for this row, so its effect is an input here).
-- The Act says nothing about what a breach of warranty entitles the recipient to; no remedy beyond "treat the contract as
-- repudiated" is encoded.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the conventions of the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage
-- table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use. See NOTES.md.

§ `Supply of Goods Act 1982 -- implied terms in transfer and hire`

§§ `1, 6, 18, 19 -- which contracts the Act reaches`

-- s 1(1): a "contract for the transfer of goods" is one under which one person "transfers or agrees to transfer to another the
-- property in goods, other than an excepted contract". s 1(2) excepted: (a) "a contract of sale of goods"; (b) "a hire-purchase
-- agreement"; (c) a transfer "made by deed and for which there is no consideration other than the presumed consideration imported
-- by the deed"; (d) "a contract intended to operate by way of mortgage, pledge, charge or other security".
-- s 1(3): it is a contract for the transfer of goods "whether or not services are also provided", and "whatever is the nature of
-- the consideration".
-- s 6(1): a "contract for the hire of goods" is one under which one person "bails or agrees to bail goods to another by way of hire,
-- other than a hire-purchase agreement".
-- The labels below are everyday descriptions; that a barter or a contractor's materials fall in s 1 and not in the Sale of Goods
-- Act is an INFERENCE from s 1(2)(a) and (3) (the Act does not define "contract of sale of goods").
DECLARE Arrangement IS ONE OF
    `a sale of goods for money`
    `a hire-purchase agreement`
    `a barter or part-exchange`
    `a contractor supplying materials with the work`
    `a gift made by deed`
    `a pledge or other security over goods`
    `a rental or hire of goods`

GIVEN `the arrangement` IS A Arrangement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a contract for the transfer of goods` IF
    CONSIDER `the arrangement`
    WHEN `a barter or part-exchange` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a contractor supplying materials with the work` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the arrangement` IS A Arrangement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a contract for the hire of goods` IF
    CONSIDER `the arrangement`
    WHEN `a rental or hire of goods` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 18(1): "goods" includes "all personal chattels (including emblements, industrial growing crops, and things attached to or
-- forming part of the land which are agreed to be severed ...) other than things in action and money".
-- That land itself is not a "personal chattel" is an INFERENCE; the Act does not say so.
DECLARE Thing IS ONE OF
    `a household appliance`
    `crops agreed to be harvested and handed over`
    `a fixture agreed to be removed from the land`
    `money`
    `a share or debt (a thing in action)`
    `the land itself`

GIVEN `the thing` IS A Thing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is goods` IF
    CONSIDER `the thing`
    WHEN `money` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a share or debt (a thing in action)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the land itself` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 19(2): "Nothing in this Act applies in relation to a contract made before 12 November 1993" (save as applicable through s 5 of
-- the Civil Law Act 1909, which is not encoded). The date is given as a number YYYYMMDD.
GIVEN `the contract date as YYYYMMDD` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act applies to a contract made on that date` IF
    `the contract date as YYYYMMDD` >= 19931112

§§ `2, 7 -- title and quiet possession`

-- s 2(1): in a transfer, "an implied condition on the part of the transferor that ... he has a right to transfer the property",
-- other than in a contract to which s 2(3) applies -- one showing "an intention that the transferor should transfer only such title
-- as he or a third person may have".
-- s 2(2)(a): an implied WARRANTY that the goods are free "from any charge or encumbrance not disclosed or known to the transferee
-- before the contract is made". s 2(4): in a limited-title contract, a warranty only "that all charges or encumbrances known to the
-- transferor and not known to the transferee have been disclosed".
-- s 7(1): in a hire, "an implied condition on the part of the bailor that ... he has a right to transfer possession of the goods by
-- way of hire for the period of the bailment". s 7 has no limited-title variant.
GIVEN `it is a hire` IS A BOOLEAN, `the contract transfers only such title as the transferor has` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the supplier had no right to transfer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the title condition is breached` IF
        `the supplier had no right to transfer`
    AND (   `it is a hire`
         OR (NOT `the contract transfers only such title as the transferor has`))

GIVEN `the contract transfers only such title as the transferor has` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the transferor knew of the encumbrance` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the encumbrance was disclosed to or known by the transferee before the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the encumbrance warranty is breached` IF
    IF `the contract transfers only such title as the transferor has`
    THEN     `the transferor knew of the encumbrance`
         AND (NOT `the encumbrance was disclosed to or known by the transferee before the contract`)
    ELSE NOT `the encumbrance was disclosed to or known by the transferee before the contract`

§§ `3-5, 8-10 -- description, quality, fitness, sample`

-- The transfer sections (3, 4, 5) and the hire sections (8, 9, 10) are worded alike except for the credit-broker limbs, which are
-- not encoded; one record serves both. "Supplier" means transferor or bailor, "recipient" transferee or bailee.
--
-- s 3(2) / 8(2): where goods are transferred or bailed "by description", "an implied condition that the goods will correspond with
-- the description". Nothing in s 3 or s 8 requires the supplier to act in the course of a business.
-- s 4(1) / 9(1): "there is no implied condition or warranty about the quality or fitness" except as these sections and ss 5, 10
-- provide.
-- s 4(2) / 9(2): where the supplier transfers or bails "in the course of a business, there is an implied condition that the goods
-- supplied under the contract are of satisfactory quality". (2A): the standard "a reasonable person would regard as satisfactory".
-- s 4(3) / 9(3): the condition "does not extend to any matter" (a) "specifically drawn to the [recipient's] attention before the
-- contract is made"; (b) "where the [recipient] examines the goods before the contract is made, which that examination ought to
-- reveal"; (c) where by sample, "which would have been apparent on a reasonable examination of the sample".
-- s 4(4)-(6) / 9(4)-(6): where the recipient makes known "any particular purpose", the goods must be "reasonably fit for that
-- purpose", unless "the circumstances show that the [recipient] does not rely, or that it is unreasonable for him to rely, on the
-- skill or judgment" of the supplier.
-- s 4(8) / 9(8): these apply to "a person who in the course of a business is acting as agent for another", "except where that other
-- is not transferring [bailing] in the course of a business and either the [recipient] knows that fact or reasonable steps are taken
-- to bring it to the [recipient's] notice".
-- s 5(2) / 10(2): by sample, implied conditions (a) "that the bulk will correspond with the sample in quality"; (b) "that the
-- [recipient] will have a reasonable opportunity of comparing the bulk with the sample"; (c) "that the goods will be free from any
-- defect, making their quality unsatisfactory, which would not be apparent on reasonable examination of the sample". Nothing in
-- s 5 or s 10 requires a business either.
-- s 18(3): quality includes "state and condition", and in appropriate cases fitness for common purposes, appearance and finish,
-- freedom from minor defects, safety and durability. Whether goods are unsatisfactory is an input here.
DECLARE Supply HAS
    `it is a hire` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contract transfers only such title as the transferor has` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the supplier had no right to transfer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the supplier acts in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a business agent supplies for a private owner` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the recipient knew or was told the owner was private` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods are of unsatisfactory quality` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the defect was drawn to the recipient's attention before the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the recipient examined the goods before the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `that examination ought to have revealed the defect` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the supply was by description` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods correspond with the description` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the supply was by reference to a sample` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the bulk corresponds with the sample in quality` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the recipient had a reasonable opportunity to compare bulk with sample` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a reasonable examination of the sample would have revealed the defect` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the recipient made a particular purpose known` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods are not reasonably fit for that purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the recipient did not or could not reasonably rely on the supplier` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the supplier has shown the recipient does not deal as consumer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the supplier has shown the breach is so slight that rejection is unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contract shows an intention that the slight-breach rule not apply` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supplier is treated as acting in the course of a business` IF
        `the supply`'s `the supplier acts in the course of a business`
    OR  (    `the supply`'s `a business agent supplies for a private owner`
         AND (NOT `the supply`'s `the recipient knew or was told the owner was private`))

GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the description condition is breached` IF
        `the supply`'s `the supply was by description`
    AND (NOT `the supply`'s `the goods correspond with the description`)

GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the satisfactory quality condition is breached` IF
        `the supplier is treated as acting in the course of a business` `the supply`
    AND `the supply`'s `the goods are of unsatisfactory quality`
    AND (NOT `the supply`'s `the defect was drawn to the recipient's attention before the contract`)
    AND (NOT (    `the supply`'s `the recipient examined the goods before the contract`
              AND `the supply`'s `that examination ought to have revealed the defect`))
    AND (NOT (    `the supply`'s `the supply was by reference to a sample`
              AND `the supply`'s `a reasonable examination of the sample would have revealed the defect`))

GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fitness for purpose condition is breached` IF
        `the supplier is treated as acting in the course of a business` `the supply`
    AND `the supply`'s `the recipient made a particular purpose known`
    AND (NOT `the supply`'s `the recipient did not or could not reasonably rely on the supplier`)
    AND `the supply`'s `the goods are not reasonably fit for that purpose`

-- s 5(2)(a) and (c) / 10(2)(a) and (c): the two sample conditions that ss 5A and 10A can downgrade.
GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a downgradable sample condition is breached` IF
        `the supply`'s `the supply was by reference to a sample`
    AND (    (NOT `the supply`'s `the bulk corresponds with the sample in quality`)
         OR  (    `the supply`'s `the goods are of unsatisfactory quality`
              AND (NOT `the supply`'s `a reasonable examination of the sample would have revealed the defect`)))

-- s 5(2)(b) / 10(2)(b): the reasonable-opportunity-to-compare condition, which ss 5A and 10A do not mention.
GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the opportunity to compare condition is breached` IF
        `the supply`'s `the supply was by reference to a sample`
    AND (NOT `the supply`'s `the recipient had a reasonable opportunity to compare bulk with sample`)

§§ `5A, 10A, 18(5) -- slight breaches by a non-consumer`

-- s 5A(1) / 10A(1): where the recipient would have "the right to treat the contract as repudiated by reason of a breach ... of a
-- condition implied by section 3, 4 or 5(2)(a) or (c)" [8, 9 or 10(2)(a) or (c)], but "the breach is so slight that it would be
-- unreasonable" to do so, "then, if the [recipient] does not deal as consumer, the breach is not to be treated as a breach of
-- condition but may be treated as a breach of warranty". (2) "unless a contrary intention appears in, or is to be implied from, the
-- contract". (3) "It is for the [supplier] to show" the breach is slight. s 18(5): "it is for the transferor or bailor claiming
-- that the transferee or bailee does not deal as consumer to show that he does not."
-- The title condition (ss 2(1), 7(1)) and the s 5(2)(b) / 10(2)(b) condition are not listed, so this rule never downgrades them.
-- That breach of a condition otherwise lets the recipient treat the contract as repudiated is the premise of s 5A(1)(a); the Act
-- does not state it as a rule of its own.
GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a downgradable condition is breached` IF
        `the description condition is breached` `the supply`
    OR  `the satisfactory quality condition is breached` `the supply`
    OR  `the fitness for purpose condition is breached` `the supply`
    OR  `a downgradable sample condition is breached` `the supply`

GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the slight-breach rule applies` IF
        `the supply`'s `the supplier has shown the recipient does not deal as consumer`
    AND `the supply`'s `the supplier has shown the breach is so slight that rejection is unreasonable`
    AND (NOT `the supply`'s `the contract shows an intention that the slight-breach rule not apply`)

GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the recipient may treat the contract as repudiated` IF
        `the title condition is breached` (`the supply`'s `it is a hire`) (`the supply`'s `the contract transfers only such title as the transferor has`) (`the supply`'s `the supplier had no right to transfer`)
    OR  `the opportunity to compare condition is breached` `the supply`
    OR  (    `a downgradable condition is breached` `the supply`
         AND (NOT `the slight-breach rule applies` `the supply`))

GIVEN `the supply` IS A Supply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the breach is treated only as a breach of warranty` IF
        `a downgradable condition is breached` `the supply`
    AND `the slight-breach rule applies` `the supply`
    AND (NOT `the recipient may treat the contract as repudiated` `the supply`)

§§ `11 -- excluding the implied terms`

-- s 11(1): a right, duty or liability arising by implication of law "may (subject to subsection (2) and the Unfair Contract Terms
-- Act 1977) be negatived or varied by express agreement, or by the course of dealing between the parties, or by such usage as binds
-- both parties". s 11(2): "An express condition or warranty does not negative a condition or warranty implied by the preceding
-- provisions of this Act unless inconsistent with it."
-- What the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 allows is an input: that Act is not encoded here.
GIVEN `an express term is inconsistent with the implied term` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a course of dealing between the parties excludes it` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a usage binding both parties excludes it` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 permits the exclusion` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the implied term is negatived` IF
        `the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 permits the exclusion`
    AND (   `an express term is inconsistent with the implied term`
         OR `a course of dealing between the parties excludes it`
         OR `a usage binding both parties excludes it`)
